![medium](https://www.gstatic.com/codereviewagent/medium-priority.svg)

The autoloader implementation is inefficient because it re-allocates the `$namespace_map` array on every single class lookup attempt in the WordPress environment. Additionally, it iterates through the map even for classes that don't belong to this plugin. 

I suggest optimizing this by:
1. Checking the base namespace prefix once before entering the loop.
2. Using a `static` variable for the map to avoid repeated allocation.
3. Using `require` instead of `require_once` for a slight performance gain, as the autoloader is only triggered if the class is not yet defined.

```php
        spl_autoload_register(function ($class) use ($plugin_dir) {
            $base_prefix = 'WPALLSTARS\\FixPluginDoesNotExistNotices\\';
            if (strncmp($base_prefix, $class, 39) !== 0) {
                return;
            }

            static $namespace_map = null;
            if ($namespace_map === null) {
                $namespace_map = array(
                    'WPALLSTARS\\FixPluginDoesNotExistNotices\\Admin\\' => 'admin/lib/',
                    'WPALLSTARS\\FixPluginDoesNotExistNotices\\'        => 'includes/',
                );
            }

            foreach ($namespace_map as $prefix => $relative_path) {
                if (strncmp($prefix, $class, strlen($prefix)) === 0) {
                    $relative_class = substr($class, strlen($prefix));
                    $file = $plugin_dir . $relative_path . str_replace('\\', DIRECTORY_SEPARATOR, $relative_class) . '.php';

                    if (file_exists($file)) {
                        require $file;
                        return;
                    }
                }
            }
        });
```

